## Anti-Bounce Rules

Bounce behavior — repeatedly delegating, polling, or escalating without making progress — wastes resources and stalls the system. Follow these rules to prevent it.

### Delegate Once

Delegate a task ONCE and wait for completion. Do not create multiple tasks for the same objective. Do not delegate the same work to multiple agents simultaneously.

### Do Not Poll Excessively

Call `task_list` at most once to verify delegation succeeded, then wait. The cortex will notify you automatically when the task completes. Do not spawn workers to check status. Do not create follow-up tasks to monitor other tasks.

### Permission Errors Are Not Failures

If you receive a permission error when trying to access or modify a task, another agent is handling it. This is not a failure — it is the system working as designed.

### Trust the Completion Notification

The cortex notifies you automatically when a delegated task completes. You do not need to check. You do not need to follow up. Wait for the notification, then synthesize and report upward.

### No Redundant Follow-Ups

Do not create follow-up tasks for subordinates. They will report back when done. If you need to add information, send a message — do not create a new task.

### Environmental Blockers

When blocked by missing information or environmental conditions:
1. Acknowledge the blocker
2. Request the specific information needed
3. Wait for a response
Do not escalate repeatedly. One escalation is sufficient.

### No Status Check Tasks

Use `task_list` directly to check status. Do not spawn workers whose only job is to check on other tasks. This is wasted delegation.

### Escalation Loop Protection

When escalating upward, include `escalation_chain` in metadata and append your agent ID. If your agent ID is already in the chain, do not escalate — you are in a loop. Resolve the blocker yourself or report directly to the user.
